<h2>Ultimate Alignment and Redemption</h2>
<div class="row">
	<section>
		<h3>Alignment Position on an Ultimate Axis</h3>
		<ul class="toggleLiVis" style="list-style-type:circle">
			<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>Time to talk about trees:</b></li>
			<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>Time to talk about trees:</b></li>
			<li style="display: none;">Regular Trees - Regular trees grant partial cover to creatures sharing its square,
				but doesn't
				otherwise affect the creature's fighting space.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Massive Trees - Massive trees take up an entire square and provide full cover to
				creatures
				behind them.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Fallen Trees - Fallen logs provide cover just as a low wall, and can be crossed by
				spending an additional 5 feet of movement. Massive fallen trees destroy the undergrowth and instead count as
				a high wall that is easy to climb and walk across.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Climb - Trees can be climbed (typically DC15), and a creature may choose to climb
				through connected squares of trees instead of moving along the ground, or along massive trees as though
				they were walls.</li>
		</ul>
		<ul class="toggleLiVis" style="list-style-type:circle">
			<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>Lets talk about the forest canopy:</b></li>
			<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>Lets talk about the forest canopy:</b></li>
			<li style="display: none;">Combat - Creatures on platforms, branches, or blinds in a forest canopy have cover
				against creatures on the ground. In medium or dense forests, or specially constructed blinds, they also have
				concealment.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Movement - Creatures can climb through the canopy as though climbing through trees,
				except that in dense forests, the canopy covers every square (unless its a clearing).</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Treehouses - Treehouse settlements are typically interconnected with platforms,
				rope bridges and ladders, and pulleys. These buildings and paths are treated as normal structures and
				terrain, though acrobatics, climb, or strength checks may be needed to traverse gaps.</li>
		</ul>
		<ul class="toggleLiVis" style="list-style-type:circle">
			<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>What about Walking through the Undergrowth?</b></li>
			<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>What about Walking through the Undergrowth?</b></li>
			<li style="display: none;">What is it? - Undergrowth refers to everything growing along the forest floor, from
				grasses and bushes to flowers and vines. They tend to hamper movement for creatures traversing the forest
				floor. In dense forests, undergrowth may extend all the way up to the canopy.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Light Undergrowth - These squares count as difficult terrain, provide concealment,
				and impose a -2 penalty to Acrobatics and Stealth checks.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Heavy Undergrowth - These squares count as very difficult (4x movement cost),
				provide improved concealment (30% miss chance), and impose a -5 penalty to Acrobatics checks and +3 to
				Stealth checks (+5-2).
			</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Trails - Pathways and trails wind through most forests, though they are rarer in
				dense forests. Creatures on a pathway ignore the effects of trees and undergrowth.</li>
		</ul>
		<ul class="toggleLiVis" style="list-style-type:circle">
			<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>Shh...</b><small><i>What about Stealth and Perception?</i></small></li>
			<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>Shh...</b><small><i>What about Stealth and
						Perception?</i></small></li>
			<li style="display: none;">Stealth - Stealth is usually easy in a forest, as the undergrowth, trees, and
				canopy can all provide cover or concealment (though they may hamper movement). Native wild creatures are
				always assumed to be using stealth while moving through forest terrain when not in combat.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Perception - The background noise of the forest increase the DC of sound-based
				perception checks by 1 per square of distance. Most forests also have a maximum visual distance where the
				foliage completely breaks line of sight.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h3>Marsh's and Swamps</h3>
		<ul class="toggleLiVis" style="list-style-type:circle">
			<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>What about Bogs?</b></li>
			<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>What about Bogs></b></li>
			<li style="display: none;">What is it? - A bog is a place of deep mud or standing water more than 1 foot deep.
				Bogs and undergrowth are mutually exclusive</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Shallow Bogs - These squares count as difficult terrain, impose a -2 penalty to
				Acrobatics and Stealth, and are too shallow to swim.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Deep Bogs - These squares count as very difficult terrain (4x movement cost) for
				walking creatures, or difficult terrain for swimming creatures. These squares make acrobatics checks
				impossible and impose a -2 penalty to stealth checks. Small or smaller creatures must swim through a bog.
			</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Fighting in a Deep Bog - Small and smaller creatures gain improved cover in a deep
				bog (30% miss chance), while medium or large creatures gain cover (though they may crouch as a move action
				to gain this improved cover). Creatures with this improved cover take a -10 penalty on attacks against
				creatures without this improved cover.</li>
		</ul>
		<ul class="toggleLiVis" style="list-style-type:circle">
			<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>What about Walking through the Undergrowth?</b></li>
			<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>What about Walking through the Undergrowth?</b></li>
			<li style="display: none;">What is it? - Undergrowth refers to anything growing in the marsh, such as bushes,
				rushes, and grasses. They tend to hamper movement for creatures traversing the marsh.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Light Undergrowth - These squares count as difficult terrain, provide concealment,
				and impose a -2 penalty to Acrobatics and Stealth checks.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Heavy Undergrowth - These squares count as very difficult (4x movement cost),
				provide improved concealment (30% miss chance), and impose a -5 penalty to Acrobatics checks and +3 to
				Stealth checks (+5-2).
			</li>
		</ul>
		<ul class="toggleLiVis" style="list-style-type:circle">
			<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>Quick! Tell Me about Quicksand!</b></li>
			<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>Quick! Tell Me about Quicksand!</b></li>
			<li style="display: none;">Spotting the Danger - Quicksand is almost invisible and hard to spot. As a move
				action, a character can inspect a single square to see if it contains quicksand (typically DC15 in open
				terrain, and DC25 in dense marshes). A creature is also entitled to a DC15 Survival check to notice
				quicksand before stepping into it. Creatures that are running, charging, or moving faster than half speed in
				combat have no chance of spotting the danger before blundering into it, and their momentum typically carries
				them 5-10 feet into the quicksand before ending their movement.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Swimming - Creatures must make a swim check every round as a full-round action
				while in quicksand. It is typically DC10 to simply tread water in place, while its typically DC15 to move 5
				feet in a desired direction. Failing this check causes them to sink 5 feet into the quicksand. Otherwise,
				swimming in quicksand is the same as swimming in unusual terrain.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Rescue - Pulling out a character trapped in quicksand is difficult, and requires a
				branch, rope, or similar tool to reach the victim. Then, they must succeed at a DC15 strength check to pull
				the victim 5 feet closer, while the victim must make a DC10 strength check to hold on, or they release the
				rope and must immediately make a DC15 swim check to not sink 5 feet below the surface.</li>
		</ul>
		<ul class="toggleLiVis" style="list-style-type:circle">
			<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>What about Hedgerows?</b></li>
			<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>What about Hedgerows?</b></li>
			<li style="display: none;">What are they? - Hedgerows are tangles of stones, soil, and thorns.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Narrow Hedgerows - These function as low walls that can be moved through at the
				cost of 3 squares of movement.</li>
			<li style="display: none;">Wide Hedgerows - These function as high walls that can be moved through at the cost
				of 4 squares of movement, or climbed through at the cost of 2 squares of movement.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</div>
